// ============================================================================
// Deep recursion and stack overflow on the 6502
// ============================================================================
//
// This example demonstrates what happens when a NON-tail-recursive function
// recurses too deeply. It COMPILES CLEANLY - stack overflow is a RUNTIME
// condition on the 6502, and the hardware has no stack-limit detection, so
// there is no compile-time error and no runtime trap. It just silently
// corrupts memory. This file exists to document that limitation and the way
// to avoid it (tail recursion / iteration).
//
// Two stacks grow with each non-tail recursive call:
//
//   1. Hardware stack ($0100-$01FF, 256 bytes) - the 6502's own stack. Each
//      JSR pushes a 2-byte return address. It overflows after ~128 nested
//      calls, at which point the stack pointer wraps and RTS starts returning
//      to GARBAGE addresses => runaway execution / crash.
//
//   2. Software stack ($0200-$02FF, 256 bytes, pointer in zero page $FF) - a
//      compiler-managed stack Wraith uses ONLY for recursion. Before a
//      recursive call it saves the callee's zero-page frame (and any binary-op
//      operand that must survive the call); it restores them afterward. It
//      overflows when the cumulative saved bytes exceed 256, at which point $FF
//      wraps and the newest saves overwrite the OLDEST ones => wrong results on
//      unwind. There is no bounds check.
//
// For `deep_sum` below, each level saves ~3 bytes to the software stack
// (1 byte of frame + a 2-byte u16 operand spill for the `n + ...`), so the
// software stack overflows first, at roughly level ~85; the hardware stack
// follows at ~128. deep_sum(200) blows through both.
//
// Note: the compiler emits a compile-time WARNING when a non-tail recursive
// function has a *large* frame (so `256 / frame_size` is a shallow depth).
// deep_sum's frame is tiny (1 byte), so it is NOT warned about here - its
// overflow comes from the hardware stack and operand spills, not frame size.
// A recursive function with big local arrays/structs would be flagged.

const OUT_LO: addr = 0x0400;
const OUT_HI: addr = 0x0401;

// NON-tail recursive: `n + deep_sum(...)` still needs `n` after the call
// returns, so this cannot be optimized to a loop - every level occupies stack.
fn deep_sum(n: u8) -> u16 {
    if n == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    return (n as u16) + deep_sum(n - 1);
}

#[reset]
fn main() {
    // 200 levels deep - overflows BOTH stacks. On real hardware/an emulator the
    // result is wrong and/or execution crashes; there is no error reported.
    let total: u16 = deep_sum(200);
    OUT_LO = total.low;
    OUT_HI = total.high;

    loop {}
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE FIX: tail recursion (accumulator pattern)
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `sum_tail` computes the same result but the recursive call is the ENTIRE
// return value, so Wraith's tail-call optimization rewrites it into a loop
// (`JMP <function>_loop_start` instead of `JSR`). It performs ZERO frame saves
// and ZERO stack growth - `sum_tail(200, 0)` and `sum_tail(60000, 0)` both run
// in constant space and never overflow.
//
//   fn sum_tail(n: u8, acc: u16) -> u16 {
//       if n == 0 {
//           return acc;
//       }
//       return sum_tail(n - 1, acc + (n as u16));
//   }
//
// Rule of thumb on the 6502: keep non-tail recursion shallow (well under ~100
// levels, fewer if frames are large), and use tail recursion or an explicit
// loop for anything that can go deep.
